sections.content:
Let us look honestly at what the naive approach produces. Here is an `Order` class managing its own status with `if` chains:

```js
class Order {
  constructor() { this.status = 'pending'; }

  pay() {
    if (this.status === 'pending') {
      this.status = 'paid';
    } else if (this.status === 'paid') {
      console.log('Already paid.');
    } else if (this.status === 'shipped') {
      console.log('Already paid — order is shipped.');
    } else if (this.status === 'cancelled') {
      console.log('Cannot pay — order cancelled.');
    }
  }

  ship() {
    if (this.status === 'pending') {
      console.log('Cannot ship — not paid.');
    } else if (this.status === 'paid') {
      this.status = 'shipped';
    } else if (this.status === 'shipped') {
      console.log('Already shipped.');
    } else if (this.status === 'cancelled') {
      console.log('Cannot ship — cancelled.');
    }
  }
  // cancel() looks identical in structure...
}
```

This design has serious structural problems:

**Shotgun surgery** — adding a fifth state (`refunded`) means editing every method. You must touch `pay()`, `ship()`, and `cancel()` even though `refunded` only interacts with one of them. The open/closed principle is violated on every addition.

**Implicit knowledge** — the allowed transitions are scattered across every method. There is no single place to look and see 'what transitions are legal from paid?' You have to mentally grep across all methods.

**Untestable in isolation** — you cannot test 'what happens when ship() is called from the paid state' without constructing a full Order object and mutating it into the paid state first. Each state's behaviour is entangled with the object.

**Accidental state corruption** — if any branch forgets to set `this.status`, the object silently moves into an inconsistent state. There is no compiler help.

The State pattern eliminates every one of these problems.

sections.code:
// ── ConcreteState classes ─────────────────────────────────────────
class PendingState {
  pay(order) {
    console.log('[Pending] Payment received.');
    order.setState(new PaidState());
  }
  ship(order)   { console.log('[Pending] Cannot ship — order not yet paid.'); }
  cancel(order) {
    console.log('[Pending] Order cancelled.');
    order.setState(new CancelledState());
  }
  name() { return 'Pending'; }
}

class PaidState {
  pay(order)  { console.log('[Paid] Already paid. No action.'); }
  ship(order) {
    console.log('[Paid] Dispatching to warehouse.');
    order.setState(new ShippedState());
  }
  cancel(order) {
    console.log('[Paid] Cancelling and issuing refund.');
    order.setState(new CancelledState());
  }
  name() { return 'Paid'; }
}

class ShippedState {
  pay(order)    { console.log('[Shipped] Already paid.'); }
  ship(order)   { console.log('[Shipped] Already dispatched.'); }
  cancel(order) { console.log('[Shipped] Cannot cancel — parcel is in transit.'); }
  name() { return 'Shipped'; }
}

class CancelledState {
  pay(order)    { console.log('[Cancelled] Order cancelled — reopen to pay.'); }
  ship(order)   { console.log('[Cancelled] Order is cancelled.'); }
  cancel(order) { console.log('[Cancelled] Already cancelled.'); }
  name() { return 'Cancelled'; }
}

// ── Context ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Order {
  #state;
  #history = [];

  constructor(id) {
    this.id = id;
    this.#state = new PendingState();
  }

  setState(newState) {
    const transition = `${this.#state.name()} -> ${newState.name()}`;
    this.#history.push({ at: Date.now(), transition });
    console.log(`  [Order ${this.id}] Transition: ${transition}`);
    this.#state = newState;
  }

  pay()      { this.#state.pay(this); }
  ship()     { this.#state.ship(this); }
  cancel()   { this.#state.cancel(this); }
  status()   { return this.#state.name(); }
  history()  { return this.#history; }
}

// ── Usage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const order = new Order('ORD-001');
console.log('Status:', order.status());  // Pending

order.ship();    // [Pending] Cannot ship — order not yet paid.
order.pay();     // Payment received -> Transition: Pending -> Paid
order.pay();     // [Paid] Already paid. No action.
order.ship();    // Dispatching -> Transition: Paid -> Shipped
order.cancel();  // [Shipped] Cannot cancel — parcel is in transit.

console.log('Final status:', order.status()); // Shipped
console.log('History:', order.history());
// [ { at: 1720000000001, transition: 'Pending -> Paid' },
//   { at: 1720000000002, transition: 'Paid -> Shipped' } ]

sections.content:
There are two schools of thought on where transition logic lives, and you will encounter both in codebases:

**State-driven (GoF canonical form):** Each ConcreteState calls `ctx.setState(new NextState())` from inside its action methods. The state class owns its own outgoing transitions. This is the model shown above. Advantage: each state file is self-documenting — you read `PaidState` and see exactly where it can go. Disadvantage: state classes need to know about sibling state classes (importing them), creating a web of interdependencies in large machines.

**Context-driven (table-driven or centralized):** The Context holds a transition table — a map of `{ [currentState]: { [event]: nextState } }` — and handles all transitions centrally. ConcreteStates do not call `setState`; they just perform their action and return. The Context looks up the next state in the table and calls `setState` itself. Advantage: the full transition graph is visible in one place; adding a new transition is a single table entry. Disadvantage: state behaviour and transition logic are separated, which can make complex guard conditions harder to express cleanly.

**Hybrid:** States implement action methods that *return* the next state (or `null` for no transition). The Context calls the method and updates itself based on the return value. This avoids the import coupling of GoF and keeps the centralization of context-driven.

```js
class PendingState {
  pay(order) {
    console.log('Payment received.');
    return new PaidState(); // return next state
  }
  ship()   { console.log('Not paid.'); return null; }
  cancel() { return new CancelledState(); }
}

class Order {
  transition(event, ...args) {
    const next = this._state[event](this, ...args);
    if (next) this._state = next;
  }
  pay()    { this.transition('pay'); }
  ship()   { this.transition('ship'); }
}
```

For small machines (under 6 states), the GoF State-driven form is clearest. For machines with 10+ states or complex guards, the hybrid or table-driven form scales better.

Real-world state machines need three refinements beyond the basic pattern:

**Guard conditions** are predicates that must be true for a transition to fire. `ship()` from `Paid` should only proceed if `order.stock > 0`. Without guards, illegal transitions need runtime errors thrown inside the state method — acceptable but clunky. With guards, the state checks the condition first:

```js
ship(order) {
  if (order.stock <= 0) {
    console.log('[Paid] Cannot ship — out of stock.');
    return; // guard fails, no transition
  }
  console.log('[Paid] Shipping.');
  order.setState(new ShippedState());
}
```

**Entry actions** are side effects fired every time a state is entered, regardless of which transition brought you there. Sending a 'Your order has shipped' email should fire whenever `ShippedState` is entered, not only from `PaidState.ship()`. The cleanest approach is an optional `onEnter(ctx)` method called by the Context's `setState`:

```js
setState(newState) {
  if (this._state.onExit) this._state.onExit(this);
  this._state = newState;
  if (this._state.onEnter) this._state.onEnter(this);
}
```

**Exit actions** fire when leaving a state — useful for cleanup (closing a database transaction, stopping a timer). Together, entry and exit actions let you centralise cross-cutting concerns (logging, events, notifications) *per state* rather than per transition, dramatically reducing duplication.

These concepts map directly to the **statechart** formalism (Harel, 1987) and are first-class features of XState — one strong reason to reach for a library when your machine reaches meaningful complexity.

sections.code:
// ── State pattern with singleton states, guards, and entry actions ─

// Singleton instances — allocated once, reused across all Orders
const States = {};

class PendingState {
  pay(order) {
    order.setState(States.Paid);
  }
  ship(order)   { console.warn('Guard: pay first.'); }
  cancel(order) { order.setState(States.Cancelled); }
  onEnter(order) { console.log(`[Order ${order.id}] Created — awaiting payment.`); }
  name() { return 'Pending'; }
}

class PaidState {
  pay(order)  { console.warn('Guard: already paid.'); }
  ship(order) {
    if (order.stock <= 0) {
      console.warn('[Paid] Guard: out of stock — cannot ship.');
      return;
    }
    order.stock--;
    order.setState(States.Shipped);
  }
  cancel(order) { order.setState(States.Cancelled); }
  onEnter(order) {
    console.log(`[Order ${order.id}] Paid — sending payment confirmation...`);
    // emailService.send(order.customerEmail, 'payment-confirmation');
  }
  name() { return 'Paid'; }
}

class ShippedState {
  pay(order)    { console.warn('Guard: already paid.'); }
  ship(order)   { console.warn('Guard: already shipped.'); }
  cancel(order) { console.warn('Guard: cannot cancel — in transit.'); }
  onEnter(order) {
    console.log(`[Order ${order.id}] Shipped — sending tracking notification...`);
  }
  name() { return 'Shipped'; }
}

class CancelledState {
  pay(order)    { console.warn('Guard: order cancelled.'); }
  ship(order)   { console.warn('Guard: order cancelled.'); }
  cancel(order) { console.warn('Guard: already cancelled.'); }
  onEnter(order) {
    console.log(`[Order ${order.id}] Cancelled — issuing refund if applicable.`);
  }
  name() { return 'Cancelled'; }
}

// Initialise singletons
States.Pending   = new PendingState();
States.Paid      = new PaidState();
States.Shipped   = new ShippedState();
States.Cancelled = new CancelledState();

// ── Context with entry action support ─────────────────────────────
class Order {
  constructor(id, stock = 5) {
    this.id = id;
    this.stock = stock;
    this._state = States.Pending;
    if (this._state.onEnter) this._state.onEnter(this);
  }

  setState(newState) {
    this._state = newState;
    if (this._state.onEnter) this._state.onEnter(this);
  }

  pay()    { this._state.pay(this); }
  ship()   { this._state.ship(this); }
  cancel() { this._state.cancel(this); }
  status() { return this._state.name(); }
}

// ── Demo ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const o = new Order('ORD-42', 1);
o.pay();    // Paid — confirmation email
o.ship();   // Shipped — tracking notification; stock: 1->0

const o2 = new Order('ORD-43', 0);
o2.pay();
o2.ship();  // Guard: out of stock — cannot ship
console.log(o2.status()); // Paid (guard prevented transition)

sections.content:
TypeScript adds valuable compile-time safety to the State pattern. Define the state interface explicitly and let the compiler verify all ConcreteStates are complete:

```typescript
interface OrderState {
  pay(ctx: Order): void;
  ship(ctx: Order): void;
  cancel(ctx: Order): void;
  onEnter?(ctx: Order): void;
  name(): string;
}

class Order {
  private _state: OrderState;

  constructor() { this._state = new PendingState(); }

  setState(state: OrderState): void {
    this._state = state;
    this._state.onEnter?.(this);
  }

  pay():    void { this._state.pay(this); }
  ship():   void { this._state.ship(this); }
  cancel(): void { this._state.cancel(this); }
  status(): string { return this._state.name(); }
}
```

With this structure, if you add a `refund()` method to the `OrderState` interface, the TypeScript compiler immediately flags every ConcreteState that has not implemented it. This is the open/closed principle enforced by the type system: the interface is *closed* (stable), and new states are *open* (add a class without editing existing ones).

For the state enum itself, prefer a **discriminated union** if you want to make the full set of states explicit at the type level:

```typescript
type OrderStateKind = 'pending' | 'paid' | 'shipped' | 'cancelled';
```

This also lets you serialize state to a database, pass it over a REST API, or match it in a `switch` where exhaustiveness checking gives you a compiler error if you forget a case.

